The behavior of each reference type is best understood by seeing it in code. The following example demonstrates strong, weak, and soft references and their interaction with the GC.

```java
import java.lang.ref.SoftReference;
import java.lang.ref.WeakReference;

public class ReferenceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Strong reference — never collected while 'strong' is in scope
        Object strong = new Object();

        // Weak reference — collected at the next GC
        WeakReference<Object> weak = new WeakReference<>(new Object());

        // Soft reference — collected only under memory pressure
        SoftReference<byte[]> soft = new SoftReference<>(new byte[10 * 1024 * 1024]);

        System.gc();  // Suggest a GC cycle

        System.out.println("Strong still alive: " + (strong != null));
        System.out.println("Weak after GC: " + weak.get());   // Likely null
        System.out.println("Soft after GC: " + (soft.get() != null));  // Still alive
    }
}
```

After `System.gc()`, the weakly referenced object is almost certainly collected because nothing else holds a strong reference to it. The softly referenced byte array survives because the JVM still has plenty of heap. Only when memory pressure mounts will the GC reclaim soft references — and only after a full GC has failed to free enough space.

This pattern is what makes `WeakHashMap` and `ThreadLocal` cleanup work correctly. When you store a value in a `ThreadLocal`, the entry is held weakly by the thread's `ThreadLocalMap`. When the thread dies, the map dies with it — preventing leaks. But beware: in long-lived threads (such as Tomcat worker threads), values stored in `ThreadLocal` can outlive any logical request, so you must call `remove()` explicitly. This is one of the most common production memory leaks in Java web applications.
